     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · BS Personal Finance · 2026-10-19 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Axis Bank Shares Fall Despite Profit Rise: What It Means for Savers</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#Banking</span> <span>#Stock Market</span> <span>#Axis Bank</span></div>
       <p>Axis Bank shares declined up to 5 percent in early morning trade on Monday, despite the private sector lender reporting a 39 percent year-on-year (Y-o-Y) increase in consolidated net profit for the second quarter of FY27 (Q2FY27). The stock opened 2 percent lower at ₹1,264.90 on the NSE and hit an intraday low of ₹1,225.30. By 10 AM, Axis Bank was trading 4 percent lower at ₹1,240, making it a top loser on both the Nifty 50 and Sensex. For the ordinary saver, this indicates that a company's stock performance isn't solely dictated by headline profit figures; other financial metrics and market sentiment play a crucial role.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-19/axis-bank-shares-fall-despite-profit-rise-what-it-means-for-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Axis Bank reported a 39.4 percent Y-o-Y rise in consolidated net profit to ₹7,704.67 crore in Q2FY27, driven by higher core income and improved asset quality. On a standalone basis, net profit rose 37.6 percent Y-o-Y to ₹7,003.28 crore from ₹5,089.64 crore. Net interest income (NII) for Q2 FY27 was ₹15,371 crore, up 5 percent quarter-on-quarter (Q-o-Q) and 12 percent Y-o-Y, with a net interest margin (NIM) of 3.36 percent. Advances grew 23 percent Y-o-Y and 9 percent Q-o-Q to ₹13,73,727 crore, while total deposits increased 6 percent Q-o-Q and 21 percent Y-o-Y. The bank's asset quality also improved, with the Gross Non-Performing Asset (GNP) ratio at 1.16 percent and Net Non-Performing Asset (NNPA) at 0.35 percent. For savers, these strong underlying numbers suggest the bank's core business is performing well, which generally indicates stability.</p><p>However, brokerages like Motilal Oswal Financial Services (MOFSL) noted that Axis Bank's elevated operating expenses (opex) led to a slight earnings miss in Q2. Strong NII growth and lower-than-expected provisions were offset by higher opex intensity and softer treasury income. Opex grew 9 percent Y-o-Y and 11 percent Q-o-Q, including a ₹2.5 billion one-time expense related to the labor code and FCNR(B) mobilization costs. This led to the Pre-Provision Operating Profit (PPoP) growing only 6 percent Y-o-Y to ₹110.8 billion, and the cost-to-income (C/I) ratio rising to 49.4 percent from 45.5 percent. NIM was adversely impacted by 11 basis points due to FCNR(B) mobilization. The management reiterated its through-cycle NIM guidance of 3.8 percent, supported by improving retail disbursements and a better balance sheet mix. For an ordinary saver, this highlights that even good banks face operational challenges that can affect profitability and, consequently, investor confidence, even if the long-term outlook remains positive.</p><p>MOFSL reiterated a 'Neutral' stance on Axis Bank but raised its FY28E earnings estimate by approximately 5 percent, factoring in rate hikes, projecting FY28E RoA/RoE to 1.6 percent/15.1 percent, and assigned a target price of ₹1,475. Elara Capital noted that the lender lagged peers on core fundamentals despite management delivering on business growth, improved asset quality, and reduced opex to assets, primarily due to declining NIMs/top line. Elara maintained that valuations remain comfortable and recommended a 'Buy' rating with an SOTP-based target price of ₹1,572, expecting earnings support from reasonable opex to assets and benign provisioning. This demonstrates that different analysts can have varied perspectives on a stock's future, influencing market movements. For individuals, these details underscore the complexity of stock valuations beyond just quarterly profits.</p><p>What to watch: The bank's ability to manage operating expenses and maintain NIMs in the coming quarters.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively summarizes the financial results and brokerage commentary, maintaining accuracy regarding the figures provided in the text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/axis-bank-shares-fall-5-despite-39-rise-in-q2-net-profit-here-s-why-126101900187_1.html" rel="noopener">BS Personal Finance</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
