     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-19 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Indonesian Fires Burn Nearly 2 Million Hectares Causing Regional Haze Crisis</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Fires in Indonesia have burned over 1.9 million hectares between January and September, according to satellite imagery analysis by Nusantara Atlas. The blazes have driven a severe regional haze crisis, prompting school closures in Malaysia and East Timor while spiking air pollution in Singapore and the Philippines. Indonesian officials rejected Malaysia's offer of assistance, stating the nation remains capable of handling the situation. Nusantara Atlas founder David Gaveau noted that conservative monitoring methods mean the true burned area is likely even higher. Singapore confirmed consecutive unhealthy air quality days have surpassed the 2015 crisis levels.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-19/indonesian-fires-burn-nearly-2-million-hectares-causing-regi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Faithfully captures the fire metrics, regional impacts, and official responses with high precision.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-indonesia-million-hectares.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
