       <h1>Japan to Launch Historic Mars Moon Sample Mission</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Japan’s space agency, JAXA, is set to launch the Martian Moons eXploration (MMX) probe on Tuesday from the Tanegashima Space Center. The mission aims to be the first to retrieve samples from Phobos, a moon of Mars, to help determine how the solar system and terrestrial planets formed. The craft is expected to reach the Martian vicinity by August of next year.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-19/japan-to-launch-historic-mars-moon-sample-mission.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The three-year mission will collect at least 10 grams of surface material to test theories regarding the moon's origin. By establishing requirements for a round trip, the project also seeks to support future crewed exploration of the red planet and study its other moon, Deimos.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the MMX mission objectives, timeline, and scientific goals while maintaining a professional tone.</p>
