     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · BBC Health · 2026-10-19 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Private firms accused of exploiting supported housing system</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#health</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Private companies are under scrutiny for exploiting the supported exempt accommodation (SEA) system, designed for vulnerable individuals like former prisoners and domestic abuse survivors. BBC Panorama found agencies using social media to lure residents while failing to provide mandated care, effectively using tenants as &quot;cash cows&quot; to secure taxpayer-funded housing benefits.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-19/private-firms-accused-of-exploiting-supported-housing-system.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Birmingham serves as the sector's epicenter, with the Department for Work and Pensions paying out £410m in the last financial year. Critics, including local MPs and charities, describe the practice as a scandal, noting that vulnerable residents are often left in unsuitable, poorly maintained properties without proper support.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise and accurate representation of the supported exempt accommodation scandal detailed in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm62y80yxw9vo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Health</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
