     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-10-19 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>River Wensum Fish Die-Off Spotlights Global Nature Protection Failures Ahead of Cop17</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Conservation</span> <span>#Biodiversity</span> <span>#Pollution</span></div>
       <p>More than 3,000 pike, roach, carp, and rudd were discovered lifeless along the River Wensum in Norwich this August, their demise attributed to heavy rainfall flushing high concentrations of pollution into waters already depleted of oxygen by heatwave conditions. Passersby reported smelling the dead fish before seeing them. This event highlights the precarious state of Britain’s rare chalk rivers, a geological accident almost unique to the country that provides habitat for species like freshwater shrimp and otters, and drinking water for millions. The 45-mile (70km) River Wensum, despite having legal protection, is in poor ecological health.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-19/river-wensum-fish-die-off-spotlights-global-nature-protectio.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Eliot Lyne, head of the Norfolk Wildlife Trust, stated, &quot;We are a world away from the crystal clear, free flowing and mineral-rich waters they could, and should be.&quot; Only 11 of the approximately 250 chalk rivers in the country have formal protection, with many suffering from sewage pollution and overextraction. This local crisis unfolds as countries gather for Cop17 in Yerevan, Armenia, to review commitments made four years ago at a Montreal summit, where nearly every government pledged to protect nature.</p><p>The 2022 Montreal agreement set ambitious targets, including protecting 30% of the planet for nature, restoring nearly a third of degraded ecosystems, and reforming $500bn (£375m) in environmentally harmful subsidies. However, governments appear poised to miss nearly all these goals, continuing a trend of never meeting a single UN nature target since the 1992 biodiversity convention. UN Secretary-General António Guterres warned on the eve of the summit that &quot;the war on nature has not ended&quot; and &quot;nature will not survive on good intentions,&quot; specifically calling out fossil fuel subsidies as &quot;subsidising suicide&quot; that are &quot;boiling our planet and are the scourge of biodiversity.&quot;</p><p>Zac Goldsmith, a former UK government minister, acknowledged a &quot;massive gap between countries agreeing to do something and actually doing it.&quot; The UK, a prominent voice for ambition in Montreal, is currently on track to meet only three of 23 targets. British conservationists point to recent policy changes, including weakened biodiversity net gain rules in England, cuts to rainforest protection funding, and the rejection of a bottom trawling ban in marine protected areas. Craig Bennett of The Wildlife Trusts criticized this, stating, &quot;For us to go around the world telling other countries that they must achieve these targets without us having a plan how to deliver them at home is a form of neocolonialism.&quot; A UK government spokesperson highlighted £7bn in funding for nature recovery and climate resilience, new water pollution powers, and the reinstatement of £6bn in international climate finance, with about a quarter allocated to nature.</p><p>What to watch: The outcomes of Cop17 and subsequent national policy implementations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the Cop17 context, river pollution event, and UK government criticisms with solid detail.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/can-countries-deliver-on-a-deal-to-save-the-worlds-plants-and-animals" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
