     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-10-19 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Small-scale fisheries drive high mortality rates for mobulid rays globally</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Global fisheries kill approximately 259,000 mobulid rays annually, with India, Indonesia, Sri Lanka, Myanmar, and Peru accounting for 87% of these deaths. India is the highest contributor at 28.2%, driven primarily by small-scale fisheries operating in shallow coastal waters where rays feed. Limited catch data has historically masked the severity of population impacts, but recent research highlights increasing fishing pressure as a primary risk. A study tracking bycatch in Tamil Nadu from 2013 to 2023 confirmed population declines across four major devil and manta ray species due to overfishing.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-19/small-scale-fisheries-drive-high-mortality-rates-for-mobulid.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise, highly dense, and accurately reflects all specific figures and findings from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/2026/10/small-scale-fisheries-a-big-threat-to-rays/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
