     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-19 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Uttar Pradesh Overhauls Tenancy Rules: Lower Fees, Stronger Protections for Renters and Landlords</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Business</span> <span>#Real Estate</span> <span>#Uttar Pradesh</span> <span>#Tenancy Laws</span></div>
       <p>The Uttar Pradesh government recently announced plans to overhaul its tenancy rules, introducing significant changes to stamp and registration fees for rent agreements. Traditionally, rental agreements for residential properties with an 11-month term do not require mandatory registration under Indian law, which is why most agreements are structured this way. Another factor contributing to unregistered 11-month agreements has been related to circle rates.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-19/uttar-pradesh-overhauls-tenancy-rules-lower-fees-stronger-pr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Ravindra Jaiswal, Minister of State for Stamp and Registration Fees, confirmed in a press conference that the UP government intends to impose a fixed stamp duty of Rs 1,000 for rent agreements where the annual rent is Rs 2 lakh. This is a substantial departure from the old system, where stamp duty and registration fees were calculated as a percentage of rent, roughly 4%, based on four times the property's circle rate. This old method often resulted in much higher stamp duty, even for modest rents, deterring many from registering their agreements or opting for verbal contracts, as noted by Malav Virani, Partner, MDP Legal.</p><p>A major issue with unregistered or verbal rent agreements is the lack of proof, which often prevents them from being used as evidence in court unless specifically permitted. Experts believe these new reforms will work in conjunction with the Uttar Pradesh Urban Premises Tenancy Regulation Act, 2021. Virani explained that the reforms aim to support the implementation of this Act by removing the cost barrier that previously discouraged agreement registration. The overarching goal is to reduce the volume of landlord-tenant disputes that end up in court due to missing or informal paperwork.</p><p>These changes are expected to benefit both tenants and landlords. For tenants, a major relief is the reduction in costs associated with stamp duty and registration fees, which will encourage them to register their agreements. &quot;Armed with a registered agreement, tenants will enjoy a strong legal standing with proof of tenure, agreed rent, and other terms being documented,&quot; Virani stated. This documentation becomes crucial if a landlord attempts an illegal eviction, disputes rent amounts, or denies the existence of the tenancy, providing stronger legal protection for tenants.</p><p>Landlords also gain from having their contractual terms formally written and registered. This protects their property against tenants refusing to vacate after the term ends, failing to pay rent, or other defaults. Formal documentation is designed to discourage unlawful practices and provide clarity for both parties. The initiative is intended to streamline the rental process, making it more transparent and legally sound for all involved, thereby fostering a more stable rental market in Uttar Pradesh.</p><p>What to watch: The implementation of the new fixed stamp duty and its impact on agreement registration rates.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft clearly explains the proposed regulatory changes and the legal implications for landlords and tenants based on the source provided.</p>
